 lesson('9.6','Entropy and the Second Law of Thermodynamics','Use entropy and heat-engine limits to explain the direction of processes.',[
 ['Entropy S','A state quantity measuring energy dispersal and the number of accessible microscopic arrangements; units J/K.'],['Reservoir','A body large enough to transfer heat with negligible temperature change.'],['Heat engine','A cyclic device absorbing heat, doing net work, and rejecting some heat.'],['Reversible process','An ideal limiting process that produces no net entropy increase in system plus surroundings.'],['Thermal efficiency η','Fraction of heat absorbed from the hot reservoir converted to net work per cycle: η = W_net/Q_H.']],
 ['ΔS_total ≥ 0 (qualitative second-law statement)','Optional extension: ΔS = Q_rev/T (isothermal reversible transfer)','Optional extension: η_Carnot = 1 − T_C/T_H (T_C: cold reservoir; T_H: hot reservoir; both in kelvin)'],[
 s('Conservation is necessary but not sufficient','The first law allows energy balances for processes that never occur spontaneously. The second law adds a direction: total entropy of a system plus its surroundings cannot decrease. A subsystem can lose entropy if the surroundings gain at least as much. Entropy is a state function; an isolated system tends toward maximum entropy at thermodynamic equilibrium. AP Physics 2 requires qualitative second-law reasoning; the numerical entropy and Carnot material below is optional enrichment.'),
 s('Optional extension: account for both reservoirs','If heat Q moves from a hot reservoir at T_H to a cold one at T_C, entropy changes are −Q/T_H and +Q/T_C. Their sum is positive for T_H > T_C. The reversible-transfer expression is a method of calculating state changes; it does not imply a real rapid transfer is reversible.'),
 s('Engine limits','A cyclic engine returns to its starting internal energy, so W_net = Q_H − Q_C, with heat magnitudes positive here. A reversible Carnot engine is the maximum-efficiency engine between two reservoirs. Real engines have lower efficiency. A refrigerator requires work to transfer heat from cold to hot; it does not violate the second law when the surroundings are included.')],
 [w('An engine absorbs 800 J and rejects 500 J each cycle. Find work and efficiency.','W_net = 800 − 500 = 300 J.','η = 300/800 = 0.375 = 37.5%.','Rejected heat is needed for a cyclic engine operating between finite-temperature reservoirs.'),w('Optional extension: find the maximum efficiency between 600 K and 300 K.','η_Carnot = 1 − 300/600 = 0.50 = 50%.','A real engine cannot exceed this limit for those reservoirs.')],
 'Do not use Celsius in efficiency ratios or claim that entropy must increase in every individual object.',
 [c('Heat flows spontaneously from a hot body to a cold one in an isolated system. What happens to total entropy?','It increases until thermal equilibrium is reached. The hot body can lose entropy while the combined system gains entropy.','Include both bodies.'),c('Can an isolated system spontaneously move away from equilibrium toward a lower-entropy state?','No. Its total entropy cannot decrease; equilibrium corresponds to maximum entropy under its constraints.','Apply the second law to the whole system.')], 'thermal','Use temperatures in kelvin. This explorer illustrates gas states; entropy depends on the complete change, and engine efficiency needs two reservoirs.')
